VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'let\'s first talk about the end result. Now, because this was a more ambitious scene, I did have to run multiple regenerations to get towards this level of quality. More on that later. But, this feels like a real animated scene.'

[11:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The action is believable, and the characters have real personality. Crucially, the generations don\'t feel disconnected from one another. There is a flow to the scene. You can\'t tell that we\'re moving from one generation to the'

[11:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'next. It just feels like one continuous story. The sky really is the limit with this process. You can develop a huge variety of different characters, locations, and scenes, and bring them all to life in a way that feels'

[11:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'professional. Training to become an artist or animator takes years, but we\'ve achieved all of this in a matter of minutes. But, the edit is where we can really take the finished product to'

[12:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a higher level. For starters, you can see that I\'ve used timeline layering here, so that each generation trails slightly into the one before and after it. And this is a clever editing trick'

[12:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'because you start to introduce the sound of the upcoming shot before the viewer actually sees it. This kind of prepares them for what to expect, so that the edit feels really smooth and [music] not in any way jarring. It\'s often called a'

[12:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'J or an L cut. You\'ll also notice there is no music baked into my generations, and this is deliberate. It comes down to how my custom skill outputs the prompts. The lack of music means that piecing'

[12:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'together separate generations is a lot easier. You can imagine that if you have different music baked into each generation, overlapping them is going to feel very chaotic, and you won\'t have that sense of consistency. If you do'

[12:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want to add music, I would suggest doing this within the edit rather than having it generated into your scenes. Now, a really simple but clever effect here inside of Premiere Pro is this posterize'

[13:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time effect, and you\'ll have a variant of this in pretty much any editing software. See dots two natively outputs at 24 frames per second, but we can use posterize time to split the frame rate'

[13:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in half, dropping it all the way down to 12 FPS. By discarding those extra frames, our result feels less smooth and that gives it the hand-animated vibe you would have noticed in projects like Into'

[13:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the Spider-Verse and Arcane. I also like to add this adjustment layer, which sharpens the footage and also adds this layer of film grain. So, whilst the edit itself has been really quite simple, hopefully you can see how we can take'

[13:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'our AI generations and add that extra step to elevate them further. With that being said, we have reached the end of today\'s tutorial. Thank you so much for sticking around and watching it all the way through. If you have learned'

